
			powwow 2.0.0-dev README

Powwow is a client program intended mainly for playing MUD.
It implements (a subset of) the telnet protocol.

Files you should have received:

	README		    This text.
	ChangeLog	    User-visible changes in each release.
	doc/powwow.md	    Complete documentation. Read this.
	doc/powwow.help	    Online help file, as plain text.
	man/powwow.6	    Man page.
	doc/demo.powwow	    A demonstrative definition file for powwow
	doc/README.follow   Documentation for the ‘follow’ mini-program.
	autogen.sh	    A shell script that creates the configure
			    script, which creates Makefiles suitable for
			    your system
	src/*.[ch]	    Sources.
	doc/Hacking	    Read this if you want to improve powwow
			    yourself.
	AGENTS.md	    The coding conventions, and how to test,
			    document and commit a change.

COMPILING

	Powwow is supported on Linux, macOS, FreeBSD, and Windows using
	WSL2, where it is built as on Linux, or Cygwin, where ‘#module’ is
	not available.  It needs a C11 compiler, GnuTLS (unless
	configured with ‘--disable-tls’), and ncurses (unless configured
	with ‘--enable-vt100’).  PCRE2, libgrapheme and zlib are used if
	available; with zlib, the names of the characters and emoji are
	installed compressed.

	On Debian and Ubuntu, including WSL2, install the packages needed
	to build powwow, and optionally PCRE2 and zlib, with:

	sudo apt install build-essential autoconf automake pkg-config \
	    libgnutls28-dev libncurses-dev
	sudo apt install libpcre2-dev zlib1g-dev

	On FreeBSD, where ncurses and zlib are part of the base system:

	pkg install autoconf automake pkgconf gnutls
	pkg install pcre2

	On Cygwin, install these packages using its setup program:

	setup-x86_64.exe -q -P gcc-core,make,autoconf,automake,pkg-config
	setup-x86_64.exe -q -P libgnutls-devel,libncurses-devel
	setup-x86_64.exe -q -P libpcre2-devel,zlib-devel

	Powwow is built using GNU autoconf scripts, it should detect any
	system dependancies it needs and set the appropriate flags.  Just
	run:

	./configure
	make

	If you do not have a ‘configure’ script, first run:

	./autogen.sh

	which also uses ‘pkg-config’, if installed, to find the libraries
	outside the default paths, such as in ‘/usr/local’ on FreeBSD or in
	‘/opt/homebrew’ on macOS.

	The powwow binary will run from any location, but there are some
	settings set at compile time that will make more sense if you use
	the ‘install’ make target to install the binaries and
	documentation together.  For example, ‘POWWOWDIR’ will default to
	the location that ‘make install’ will put the online powwow
	documentation, making the ‘#help’ feature work out of the box
	without modifying any settings or flags.  Run from the build
	directory, as ‘src/powwow’, it uses the documentation in the source
	tree.

	If you require changes to the build process, please report the
	problems (and hopefully solution) at https://github.com/MUME/powwow.
	Also please include your system information (‘uname -a’, etc)

	There are also some other options that may be turned on or off or
	forced using configure.	 Try ‘configure --help’ to see the full
	list of compile-time configuration options.

LAUNCHER

	The directory ‘launcher’ has a desktop program, ‘powwow-launcher’, that
	lists the MUDs, one definition file each, in an order of your own, by
	dragging, adds, imports (copies of older definition files, which it
	converts), changes, duplicates and removes them, shows the definitions
	in their files, such as the aliases and actions, edits the settings for
	all of them, and starts powwow in a terminal for the one picked: in
	Windows Terminal on Windows, through the profile of powwow that the
	installer writes; on macOS in iTerm2 if installed, or else in Terminal;
	elsewhere in the terminal of ‘TERMINAL’, or one found, such as
	‘x-terminal-emulator’ or ‘xterm’.  Its settings page can choose another
	one on macOS and elsewhere, or a command, where ‘%c’ is the command
	that runs powwow, such as:

	open -na Ghostty --args -e %c

	Given a definition file, it starts powwow with it without showing its
	window.  A MUD added with “MUME handshake” gets ‘#option +identify’,
	which tells MUME that powwow edits texts locally.  The settings page
	also picks the highlighting of typed text and of the messages of
	powwow, ‘#hilite input’ and ‘#hilite messages’, for all MUDs but those
	with their own ‘#hilite’, which Highlighting picks when adding or
	editing a MUD.

	It needs Qt 6.8 or later, with Qt Quick (the module ‘qtdeclarative’),
	CMake and a C++ compiler, which these install:

	macOS         xcode-select --install
	              brew install cmake qtdeclarative     (Homebrew)
	Debian 13, Ubuntu 26.04 or later
	              apt install cmake g++ qt6-declarative-dev
	Fedora        dnf install cmake gcc-c++ qt6-qtdeclarative-devel
	Arch Linux    pacman -S cmake gcc qt6-declarative
	FreeBSD       pkg install cmake qt6-declarative

	Where the Qt of the system is older, such as in Debian 12 and Ubuntu
	24.04, aqtinstall (see WINDOWS INSTALLER) or the installer of Qt
	installs a newer one.

	After configure, ‘make launcher’ builds it in ‘launcher/build’, or
	rebuilds it, and ‘make clean’ removes it; if CMake does not find Qt,
	give where it is:

	make launcher CMAKE_FLAGS=-DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH=/opt/homebrew

	Without configure, CMake builds it:

	cmake -S launcher -B launcher/build
	cmake --build launcher/build

	On Windows it is built with the MinGW or MSVC build of Qt, not
	Cygwin's, and looks like a Windows 11 app.  On Cygwin, ‘make launcher’
	builds it with the MinGW build of Qt in ‘launcher-win’, as for the
	installer (see WINDOWS INSTALLER).

	It reads and saves the settings by running powwow, with ‘--config’ and
	‘--get’ or ‘--set’, so it needs the powwow of this source tree: run
	from its build directory, it takes the powwow built in the same tree,
	with the manual of the source tree, and otherwise it finds powwow next
	to itself, in ‘PATH’, or in ‘/opt/homebrew/bin’, ‘/usr/local/bin’ and
	the like; its settings page can choose another one.  On Windows it
	always takes the powwow installed with it.  The definition files are in
	‘POWWOWDIR’, if set, or else in the folder ‘powwow’ of the Documents
	folder on Windows, in ‘~/Library/Application Support/powwow’ on macOS
	(‘~/.config/powwow’ if that exists), and in ‘$XDG_DATA_HOME/powwow’
	(‘~/.local/share/powwow’) elsewhere.  The launcher keeps its own
	settings, such as the powwow it runs and the order of the MUDs, in
	‘$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/powwow/launcher.conf’
	(‘~/.config/powwow/launcher.conf’), in
	‘~/Library/Preferences/com.powwow.launcher.plist’ on macOS, and in the
	registry, ‘HKEY_CURRENT_USER\Software\powwow\launcher’, on Windows.

	On Windows, it also sets the lines of scrollback of the profile of
	powwow in Windows Terminal, in the fragment that the installer writes,
	‘Fragments\powwow\powwow.json’ in
	‘%LOCALAPPDATA%\Microsoft\Windows Terminal’, when powwow is installed
	for the user only; installing it again sets it back to 10000.

WINDOWS INSTALLER

	On Cygwin, powwow can be built into a directory, ‘powwow-win’, with
	the Cygwin DLLs it needs, ‘sh’, ‘nano’, and the CA certificates,
	which runs without Cygwin installed, and into a Windows installer of
	that directory, with the prefix ‘/usr’ or ‘/usr/local’, the default:

	./configure
	make windows-bundle             (just the directory powwow-win)
	make windows-installer          (also powwow-VERSION-setup.exe)

	A -dev version adds the commit it is built from, as ‘#ver’ shows it:
	‘powwow-1.3.2-dev-ebae0231-setup.exe’.

	The bundle needs the license files of the Cygwin packages that it
	has, which are only in their source packages: so the first build of
	it downloads those, about 200 MB, which needs an internet connection,
	and extracts their licenses.  Later builds in the same build
	directory download only those of packages that are new.  Likewise,
	the first build with a version of Qt gets the license texts of its
	modules from their sources, with git.

	The setup program of Cygwin installs the packages that these need,
	to build powwow, to bundle it and to get the licenses, from the
	folder that has it in the Windows command prompt:

	setup-x86_64.exe -q -P gcc-core,make,autoconf,automake,pkg-config ^
	    -P libgnutls-devel,libpcre2-devel,libncurses-devel,zlib-devel ^
	    -P nano,ca-certificates,xz,zstd,bzip2,lzip,git

	The bundle has the launcher (see LAUNCHER above), which
	‘cygwin/launcher.sh’ builds with the MinGW build of Qt 6.8 or later
	for Windows, not that of Cygwin, and its MinGW, CMake and Ninja,
	such as these, which aqtinstall
	(https://github.com/miurahr/aqtinstall) installs in ‘C:\Qt’:

	aqt install-qt windows desktop 6.10.3 win64_mingw -O C:\Qt
	aqt install-tool windows desktop tools_mingw1310 ^
	    qt.tools.win64_mingw1310 -O C:\Qt
	aqt install-tool windows desktop tools_cmake -O C:\Qt
	aqt install-tool windows desktop tools_ninja -O C:\Qt

	It takes the newest Qt in ‘C:\Qt’, or the one of ‘QT’, such as
	‘make windows-installer QT=C:/Qt/6.10.3/mingw_64’; ‘make launcher’
	builds just the launcher, in ‘launcher-win’.

	The installer needs Inno Setup 6.3 or later
	(https://jrsoftware.org/isinfo.php); ‘cygwin/installer.sh’ finds its
	compiler, ‘ISCC.exe’, where Inno Setup is installed, or set ‘ISCC’
	to its path.

	The installer is written to the build directory.  It installs powwow
	with a Start menu shortcut that opens the launcher, which lists the
	MUDs, one definition file each, in the folder ‘powwow’ of the
	Documents folder, such as ‘C:\Users\name\Documents\powwow’, and runs
	powwow for one in Windows Terminal, which the installer requires,
	through ‘cygwin/powwow-start’.  Unless unchecked, opening a ‘.powwow’
	file runs powwow with it as the definition file, through the
	launcher.  Installing a newer version updates powwow in its folder:
	the folders of the bundle are cleared first, and the MUDs and the
	settings, which are elsewhere, stay.  The icon, ‘icons/powwow.ico’,
	is a placeholder made by ‘icons/make-icon.sh’.

	The programs and libraries of Cygwin bundled are under their own
	licenses, as are Qt and the libraries of MinGW of the launcher.  The
	bundle has their license files, which ‘LICENSES.txt’ lists with where
	to get their source; the installer and ‘#help copyright’ show it.
	‘cygwin/update-licenses.sh’ downloads the source packages to
	‘cygwin-sources’ in the build directory, and keeps the licenses in
	‘cygwin-licenses’ there, and ‘cygwin/launcher.sh’ those of Qt in
	‘qt-licenses’.  ‘make update-cygwin-licenses’ updates those of
	Cygwin.  ‘make clean’ removes ‘powwow-win’, the installer and
	‘launcher-win’, where the launcher is built, and
	‘make maintainer-clean’ also these three directories.

	The launcher runs ‘cygwin/powwow-start’, which runs powwow with
	‘--config’, so that the settings for all MUDs, such as the editor,
	are saved in the user-wide settings file,
	‘%APPDATA%\powwow\powwow.conf’, and with ‘--windows’: file names are
	written as on Windows, such as ‘C:\Users\Me\Documents\mud.powwow’ or
	‘%USERPROFILE%\mud.powwow’, and the commands of ‘#!’, of
	‘#var $x=!...’ and the like, and of the editor and the pager, are
	commands of ‘cmd.exe’.  It also sets ‘TMPDIR’ to the temporary
	folder of Windows.  With ‘--windows’, unless the settings or the
	environment choose others, the shell is ‘cygwin/powwow-cmd’, which
	runs ‘cmd.exe’ in UTF-8, with each command as a line of a batch file,
	where ‘%NAME%’ is expanded and a ‘%’ is written ‘%%’; the editor is
	Notepad, in another window, or ‘nano’ in an ssh session; and the
	pager is the same, with ‘nano -v’, which only views.

	To edit with another program, give its command as in ‘cmd.exe’, but
	with each ‘\’ in double quotes written ‘\\’, as in ‘sh’ and in the
	strings of powwow, also in an ‘#alias’; the editor gets the path of
	the file after it, and the description of the text from the MUD in
	‘%TITLE%’:

	#option editor=&"C:\\Program Files\\Notepad++\\notepad++" -multiInst

	The program must not end before the text is saved and closed, or
	powwow ends the editing session at once: Notepad++ needs ‘-multiInst’,
	which opens a window of its own instead of passing the file to one
	that is open already.

INSTALLING

	If you want powwow's definition files to go in a specific directory,
	set the environment variable ‘POWWOWDIR’ to that directory. It will be
	searched first for definition files at startup.

	Put the file ‘powwow.6’ in a suitable directory if you want users on
	your system to be able to read the long and comprehensive man page ;)

	Powwow supports the MUME editing protocol.  It enables you to edit
	texts locally with your favourite editor.  So far, only MUME supports
	this protocol, but this might change in the future.

	Powwow looks for the editor in the ‘POWWOWEDITOR’ enviroment
	variable, and if it doesn't exist, in ‘EDITOR’, and uses ‘emacs’ as
	default.  For text viewing, powwow looks for ‘POWWOWPAGER’, then
	‘PAGER’, then uses ‘more’ as default; ‘#help’ looks for
	‘POWWOWHELPPAGER’ first.  A pager of ‘-’ (a hyphen) is none: the
	texts are printed instead.

	If you are on a multi-window terminal (such as an X terminal or NCSA
	telnet from a Mac or PC), powwow will let you start the editor in
	another window and remain active.  You will then also be able to edit
	several texts at the same time.	 To achieve this, let the first
	character of your editor/pager be ‘&’ (which is then skipped).

	‘#option editor=<command>’, ‘pager=<command>’,
	‘help-pager=<command>’ and ‘shell=<command>’ come before these
	variables, and are saved.  ‘#!’ runs its command with ‘/bin/sh’, or
	gives it to the shell in ‘POWWOWSHELL’, such as ‘zsh -c’, as its
	last argument; so do ‘#var $x=!<command>’ and the other commands
	that run a command after ‘!’.

	Example: (Bourne shell syntax)

	POWWOWEDITOR=vi		  Use vi as normal editor (same window as
				  powwow)
	POWWOWEDITOR='&emacs'	  Use emacs as editor in a separate window (if
				  you are on an X terminal)
	POWWOWEDITOR='&xterm -e vi'  Use vi in another X window
	POWWOWPAGER='&xterm -e less' Use less as your file viewer in another
				     X window

	If you use a multi-window terminal and have ‘emacsclient’ installed,
	it is a good choice, since you only need one emacs window for all
	your editing sessions and you don't have to start and exit emacs all
	the time.  Set ‘POWWOWEDITOR='&emacsclient'’ in that case, and make
	sure your emacs has a running server (put ‘(server-start)’ in your
	‘.emacs’ file or type ‘M-x server-start’).

	If you want to use the editing functions, you must execute the
	‘#identify’ command to notify the server that the client supports the
	protocol: ‘#option +identify’ does it each time powwow connects.  This
	can also be done with an action; see the documentation for details.

	‘make install’ also installs the completion of the arguments of
	powwow in bash, from ‘powwow --completion bash’, in
	‘$(datadir)/bash-completion/completions’, where the package
	bash-completion loads it by itself; configure with
	‘--with-bash-completion-dir=DIR’ to install it in DIR instead, or
	with ‘--without-bash-completion-dir’ not to.  Without
	bash-completion, load it in ‘~/.bashrc’ with
	‘eval "$(powwow --completion bash)"’, and in zsh, in ‘~/.zshrc’
	after ‘compinit’, with ‘eval "$(powwow --completion zsh)"’.

BUGS

	If you have any problems with powwow, try to fix them yourself
	(you have the source, right?) or at least isolate the bugs so
	the maintainer has a fair chance of fixing them. Don't expect
	a bug to be fixed, unless you explain it in detail (expecially
	how to reproduce it) and supply the powwow and OS version,
	as well as the compilation options.
	Suggestions for improvements are also very welcome.

AUTHORS OF CANCAN

	Powwow is based on cancan, by Mattias Engdegård, Finn Arne Gangstad,
        David Gay, Gary Dezern, Lai-Chang Woo, Thuzzle and others, which is no
        longer maintained.

AUTHORS OF POWWOW

	Massimiliano Ghilardi <max@Linuz.sns.it>, alias Cosmos, is the original
	author, and Gustav Hållberg contributed several new features.
	Steve Slaven contributed as the maintainer from 2005.

	Powwow is maintained at https://github.com/MUME/powwow

